Vendored third-party code: Pretext

This directory holds a single-file ESM bundle of a third-party library that the
published site executes. Everything needed to audit it is recorded here, and
`web/test/command-wizard.test.mjs` pins the bundle's digest so an unreviewed
swap of the file fails CI.

Upstream project   https://github.com/chenglou/pretext
License            MIT (full text in pretext.LICENSE)
Package            @chenglou/pretext
Version            0.0.8
Upstream commit    a79a6a595d7ea8a07874637b5234ee255b6a4a14
npm tarball        https://registry.npmjs.org/@chenglou/pretext/-/pretext-0.0.8.tgz
npm integrity      sha512-yqm2GMxnPI7VHcHwe84P8ZF0JK/2d2DMKPqMN+s95jQhwDMYYXKVFVJUMEaVWckQStdsjdLav/0Vu+d9YbtGxA==

Vendored artifact  pretext.js
sha256             c85e755d86d33718e66978d18a2733eb5d11cf44747f58846bd390ed0d6f24e7

`pretext.js` is the package's default entry (`dist/layout.js`) bundled with its
relative imports into one file so the page can load it without a package
manager or a bundler step. It is not byte-identical to any single upstream
file, so verify it in two steps instead:

  # 1. Confirm the upstream package is the one recorded above.
  curl -sSL -o /tmp/pretext.tgz "https://registry.npmjs.org/@chenglou/pretext/-/pretext-0.0.8.tgz"
  echo "sha512-$(openssl dgst -sha512 -binary /tmp/pretext.tgz | openssl base64 -A)"

  # 2. Confirm the vendored bundle is the one this repo reviewed.
  shasum -a 256 web/vendor/pretext.js

Changing the bundle means updating the digest above and in the test, which
makes the swap a reviewable diff rather than a silent one.
